## Questions and Summarized Answers

🧩 **Follow-up on the dedicated ECG mode PR — how should the ECG grid layout (12×1, 3×2, etc.) be structured? Does it need a separate interface that we can extend into ECG-specific classes?** ([OHIF/Viewers#6068](https://github.com/OHIF/Viewers/issues/6068))

* **Current PR status**

  * PR is on the back burner due to 3.13 release cleanup; ~2 weeks before the team can dedicate review time
  * PR is too large to review effectively — needs to be broken into smaller pieces (2–3 PRs)
  * Before breaking it down, the architectural approach needs a more detailed discussion so the design doesn't have to change repeatedly

* **The four-layer architecture the ECG mode needs to fit into**

  * **Cornerstone viewport** — draws the basic data (ECG traces, grid); minimal, primitive
  * **Cornerstone tools library** — adds a small amount of interaction on top of viewports (annotations, overlays)
  * **OHIF tools layer** — coordinates sets of viewports and layouts without constraining to a specific setup
  * **OHIF modes layer** — the opinionated view of how the tools should be assembled for a given workflow (this is where the ECG mode itself lives)
  * Getting the right functionality at each layer is the tricky part, especially for a new data type that doesn't behave like imaging data

* **What belongs in the ECG viewport**

  * Only camera / canvas logic and the drawing of ECG traces (and possibly the grid)
  * A simple structure specifying trace areas — e.g., "this trace goes in this region, from here to here, at this height, with this scaling"
  * Areas should be specifiable externally, either via named presets (using percentages) or by fully defined regions
  * These should live in the same properties surface as flip, rotate, etc. so they're consistent with existing viewport behavior
  * May end up needing a different camera type; that's acceptable

* **What belongs outside the viewport**

  * QRS points, heart rate, and all ECG-specific calculations — separate calculation module, imported into the viewport
  * Grid layout logic (12×1, 3×2, etc.) — should be an annotation tool or a layout mechanism on top of the viewport, not baked into it
  * This mirrors how imaging viewports work: the viewport shows the data, and tools/annotations layered on top change what the user sees

* **Why this matters**

  * If too much ECG-specific logic ends up in the viewport itself and the design changes later, it will look drastically different in a final revision and require backwards-compatibility work
  * A layered separation lets the external interface evolve (different layouts, different tools) without changing the base viewport

* **Suggested next step**

  * Before re-submitting PRs, write up the proposed approach as a design document and send to Bill, Alireza, and James
  * The team will prioritize reviewing the document so the PR isn't blocked for long
  * Bill's framing: going through this design step feels slower but is faster overall than re-implementing after each design correction

